## The core-library rule (ImplementationPlan.md 5.11.3, M21.a item 6; texts
## of 5.11.9): the core library is the unique reference that defines
## top-level System.Object and has no AssemblyRef row (Roslyn's
## IndexOfCorLibrary). Every link with a module initializer or a Main needs
## one, so even a program that uses no BCL member fails without it:
## - none: only a facade (FakeFacade forwards System.Object to FakeCore and,
##   having AssemblyRefs, never qualifies), only a non-core assembly, or a
##   candidate with an AssemblyRef (IlasmCore defines System.Object, but
##   ilasm gave it an AssemblyRef to mscorlib) is
##   'the references contain no core library (an assembly that defines System.Object and references no other assembly); given: <list>',
##   where <list> (section 12 row 2026-10-26, '5.11.9 clarified') names each
##   reference that defines or forwards top-level System.Object, as its
##   identity '<name> <version> [<token>]' followed by its note
##   ('(facade, forwards System.Object to <name>)', '(defines System.Object
##   but references <names>)' or '(defines System.Object with a base type)'),
##   and leaves out every other reference; only when no reference defines or
##   forwards System.Object is every reference named, with no note; the list
##   is in identity order whatever the command-line order (section 12 row
##   2026-10-26, 'Row 5.11.9 clarified ... corrected': the identity text
##   compared ignoring case, then exactly, then the normalized path);
## - two: FakeCore and an mscorlib that defines its own System.Object is
##   'the references mix two core libraries: <identity> (<path>) and <identity> (<path>)',
##   in the same identity order;
## - three or more (section 12 row 2026-10-26, '5.11.9 clarified (three or
##   more candidates)'): CoreA, CoreB and CoreC (Inputs/fakepack/MiniCore.il)
##   are 'the references mix <n> core libraries: <list>', <n> in digits and
##   every candidate as '<identity> (<path>)' in the same identity order,
##   joined by ', ' with ' and ' before the last;
## - IlasmCore next to FakeCore is no second core library: FakeCore is the
##   unique one and the link succeeds (System.Object is still defined twice,
##   which only matters to a link that binds it, bcl-types.test);
## - nor is BaseCore, which has no AssemblyRef but whose System.Object
##   extends the class Fake.Root (gen.py patches its TypeDef table): alone it
##   is the no-core-library error with the note '(defines System.Object with
##   a base type)', and next to FakeCore the link succeeds;
## - a --reference that adds no file still turns the built-in table off
##   (section 12 row 2026-10-26, '5.11.7 clarified ('the link has no
##   reference' ...)'): an empty directory, a directory holding only a native
##   DLL, or only a toolchain library (skipped with its warning) is the error
##   with the list 'no references', and no output is written; a
##   --print-bcl-binding request then prints that error, not the built-in
##   table's 'pass --reference=<reference assemblies>' text.
## ret42.o's main returns 42 and uses nothing from the BCL; fence.o calls
## Interlocked::MemoryBarrier.

# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj ret42.ll -o ret42.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj fence.ll -o fence.o
